  "response": "For much of the twentieth century, the Clovis-first model held that the Clovis people were the first inhabitants of the Americas. Its authority rested on a coherent archaeological signature. Distinctive fluted projectile points, repeated associations with mammoth and bison kills, and well-stratified sites across the North American interior all pointed the same way. So did a chronology in which the earliest Clovis artifacts date to roughly 13,000 calendar years ago. Any claim of older occupation had to overcome a strong prior. Reviewers demanded unambiguous artifacts, secure stratigraphic context, and radiocarbon dates free of contamination. For decades, every candidate site failed at least one of those tests, and critics dismissed each in turn as a product of bad samples or misread geofacts.\n\nThe decisive challenge came from the far end of the hemisphere. Monte Verde is located in southern Chile. Tom Dillehay, who led the excavations at Monte Verde, came to the site from a North American academic tradition shaped by the Clovis consensus. Excavation at Monte Verde began in 1977. The peat bog that sealed the deposits preserved organic remains rarely seen at open-air sites: wooden foundations, knotted cordage, hide fragments, and medicinal plants. Set against the Clovis horizon of roughly 13,000 years, the main occupation layer at Monte Verde was dated to about 20,000 years ago.\n\nThe dispute over the site could therefore be stated starkly. Either the human occupation at Monte Verde is older than 13,000 calendar years, or it is no older than 13,000 calendar years. The two readings cannot both be true, and one of them must hold. In 1997, two decades after those excavations had begun, a group of experts that included C. Vance Haynes visited Monte Verde. Haynes had long been among the most rigorous defenders of Clovis priority. The visitors examined the stratigraphy, the artifact collections, and the dating program at first hand. Despite the younger reading of the occupation, the 1997 expert panel concluded that Monte Verde was a genuine pre-Clovis site. In doing so, the panel formally determined that the human occupation at Monte Verde is older than 13,000 calendar years, settling the either-or dispute in favor of the older reading. The site's pre-Clovis antiquity follows because Monte Verde is about 1,000 years older than the oldest Clovis sites. In other words, the oldest Clovis sites are roughly a millennium younger than Monte Verde. That interval is modest in absolute terms, but it was decisive, since a single secure site predating Clovis was all the hypothesis could not accommodate.\n\nThe verdict on Monte Verde also changed how other contested localities were evaluated. Meadowcroft Rockshelter is located in Ohio. At least one of two flaws, perhaps both, affected its deepest levels. In the first, the rockshelter's position in coal-bearing country caused the early radiocarbon samples from Meadowcroft to be contaminated by coal-derived carbon. In the second, the deepest artifacts at Meadowcroft were displaced downward from younger layers. Meadowcroft thus illustrates the evidentiary standard the field applied. A site could be taken as pre-Clovis only when its chronology withstood challenges both to sample integrity and to stratigraphic position. Because Meadowcroft never fully satisfied that standard, it could not resolve the debate on its own. Its significance grew, instead, as Monte Verde established that an early occupation of the continent was plausible rather than anomalous.\n\nThe long dispute was settled less by an accumulation of ambiguous claims than by one site whose context survived hostile scrutiny. The archaeological community ultimately determined that the Clovis-first model had been falsified. That determination resolved the standoff between the younger reading of the Monte Verde occupation and the panel's judgment, in favor of the panel. Once that verdict was in place, the field turned to new questions: where the first populations entered the hemisphere, whether they followed a Pacific coastal route, and how quickly they spread south. The burden of proof had shifted. A pre-Clovis claim was no longer presumed wrong until shown otherwise, and Clovis itself came to be understood as a later regional development rather than the founding culture of the Americas.",
